tive degree",5],[141920,"empfindlichsten","adj","weak/mixed all-case plural superlative degree",6]],"inflections":[["empfindlichsten","empfindlich","genitive,masculine,singular,strong,superlative,without-article"]]},"eupen":{"entries":[["eupen",null,"/ˈɔʏ̯pən/","From Middle Dutch Oepen, Oipen, Oupen, Open. According to the prevalent theory from Proto-West Germanic *opan (“open”). The Middle Dutch spellings indeed seem to point to a back vowel; on the other hand there is no certainty about this and the modern front-rounded vowel would remain unexplained. An alternative theory derives it from *up (“up, at the top”) + *-īn. This explains the umlaut, but is still at odds with the dialectal pronunciation [ˈøˑʏ̯pə] ~ [ˈøːpə], which would require West-Germanic au-. Therefore a third theory postulates an underlying *Aupīn, in which *aup- would be a blend of *auwju (“floodplain”) with the hydronym *apa-.\nThe spelling in Eu- appears in the 16th century and is probably a Franco-Flemish representation of [øː]. The contemporary Standard German pronunciation may to some degree be a spelling pronunciation, but it also approaches the local [øˑʏ̯], which moreover generally corresponds to standard eu, äu."]],"senses":[[675065,"eupen","name","a town in German-speaking Community, province of Liège, Belgium",0]]},"feinsinnigstem":{"entries":[["feinsinnigstem",null,null,null]],"senses":[[639095,"feinsinnigstem","adj","strong dative masculine/neuter singular superlative degree of feinsinnig",0]],"inflections":[["feinsinnigstem","feinsinnig","dative,masculine,singular,strong,superlative,without-article"]]},"galliumreicher":{"entries":[["galliumreicher",null,null,null]],"senses":[[237618,"galliumreicher","adj","comparative degree of galliumreich",0],[237619,"galliumreicher","adj","inflection of galliumreich:",1],[237620,"galliumreicher","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular",2],[237621,"galliumreicher","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular",3],[237622,"galliumreicher"